  "full_response": "Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) are a class of deep neural networks that particularly well-suited for processing image data. Here is a high-level overview of how they typically process this kind of data:\n\n1. **Input Layer**: The input layer takes a multi-dimensional array representing the image. Commonly, this is a 3D array with dimensions corresponding to the width height, and color channels (e.g., RGB).\n\n2. **Convolutional Layers**: These layers apply a series of filters (also known as kernels) to the input image. filter slides (or convolves) over the image, performing an element-wise multiplication with the pixel values underneath and summing the result. This operation helps in extracting features from the image. Filters can detect patterns such as edges, textures, and more complex structures.\n\n3. **Activation Function (ReLU)**: The result of the convolution operation is passed through an activation function, most commonly the Rectified Linear Unit (ReLU). The ReLU function introduces non-linearity into the model, which is essential for learning complex patterns.\n\n4. **Pooling Layers**: These layers reduce the spatial dimensions (width and height) of the feature maps, thereby reducing the number of parameters and computations in the network. Max pooling (often performing 2x2 max pooling) is a common operation.\n\n5. **Fully Connected Layers (Dense Layers)**: After several convolutional and pooling layers, the high-level features are flattened into a 1D array and passed through one or more fully connected (dense) layers. These layers are responsible for the final classification or regression task.\n\n6. **Output Layer**: The output layer produces the final prediction. This could be a probability distribution over different classes (in cases of multiclass classification) or a single value (in cases of regression).\n\nThe process of a CNN can be summarized in the steps:\n\n1. Convolution -> ReLU -> Max Pooling\n2. Convolution -> ReLU -> Max Pooling\n3. Convolution -> ReLU -> Max Pooling\n4. ...repeating certain layers as needed)\n5. Flatten -> Fully Connected Layer(s) -> Output Layer\n\nBy applying this sequence of operations, CNNs are able to automatically and hierarchically extract and learn important features from the raw pixel data, making them highly effective for image recognition and other computer vision tasks. The combination of convolutional and pooling layers reduces the chance of overfitting and allows the network to learn more abstract representations",
